From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] OPENBOOK: translator stats and a stable region identity

berberis.stats=1 (or BERBERIS_STATS=1) turns on process-wide translator
counters, relaxed atomics in one self-describing block (magic OBSTAT2:
count, names, enabled, owner pid, baseline flag, counters, baseline,
names). Init logs the block's address, and a root shell reads it from
/proc/<pid>/mem whenever it wants, so a running app needs no extra
thread. No thread on purpose: the native bridge also initializes in
child zygotes, and ART won't fork a zygote with more than one thread. A
guest exit_group (a program run through the binfmt runner) logs a last
"openbook-stats v1" line. Off by default: each counting site then costs
one load of a flag, and no clock is read.

Counted: regions installed per tier (published in the translation cache
only), results discarded because the region was invalidated while it
was translated, accumulated elapsed compile time per tier (failed and
discarded attempts included), guest and host bytes per tier, gear-up
requests and how each heavy attempt ended (heavy code emitted, prefix
with a loop, declined as too small, bailed, no code-pool memory),
interpreter batches, invalidation requests (total, by IC IVAU, by
executable mappings going away, bytes, time), translation-cache entries
invalidated, in-flight translations thrown away, dispatcher flushes and
the threads each flush asks to return. SetTranslatedAndUnlock now says
whether it published.

Inheritance: a pthread_atfork child handler makes a fork() child (an
app zygote's) the owner of the counts and keeps what it inherited as its
baseline. A raw clone child (a guest's fork) runs no handler and keeps
the parent as owner, which a reader sees.

The berberis.profiling perf map now names each region of a mapped file
with its offset in the file too (_0x<addr>+<size>@0x<offset>), which
identifies the same region across launches where the address does not.
Anonymous and deleted mappings get no offset: they have no stable
identity. MapsSnapshot keeps the offset. The map belongs to one process:
its descriptor is kept with the pid it was opened for, and a fork or
clone child closes the inherited one and opens perf-<child>.map once,
lock-free, instead of writing into its parent's map. A failed open is
remembered for that process only.

Tests: runtime/arm64/translator_stats_test.cc drives the real entry
points: a first-gear install, one gear-up per heavy outcome, a result
invalidated before publication (lite, and heavy emitted but discarded),
a non-executable pc, IC IVAU over a translated and an untranslated line,
off counts nothing, exact totals from 8 threads, the block read back
through /proc/self/mem, counts owned by a fork child with its baseline
and kept by the parent for a raw fork child, the perf map reopened by a
fork child with the inherited descriptor closed first, a failed open
remembered per process, only file-backed regions named with an offset,
and the file offset MapsSnapshot records.
